# Everything here is optional except DATABASE_URL, and every optional setting can
# also be changed in the dashboard's Settings tab without a restart. The environment
# is the fallback, which is what you want for a deploy that has to come up already
# configured. A value saved in Settings wins over the same value set here.
#
# `docker compose up -d` sets DATABASE_URL and SESSION_DIR for the container itself,
# so this file is mainly for running the app outside Docker.

# Postgres. This is the URL `docker compose up -d db` serves.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:wa@localhost:5439/wa_outreach

# Credentials now live in Postgres, in the `wa_auth` table, so this directory is only
# read once to import a session created by an older version. Whichever holds them,
# those credentials ARE the logins for your numbers: anyone who copies them can send
# as you, and losing them means scanning every QR code again.
#
# Because the session moved into the database, a database dump is now a dump of your
# WhatsApp logins. Treat `pg_dump` output like a password file, and note that
# `docker compose down -v` destroys the credentials along with the data.
SESSION_DIR=./sessions

# Port the server listens on. The Docker image and the npm scripts both use 7070.
# PORT=7070

# Set it and the dashboard asks for a password once, then keeps a signed cookie.
# A password set in Settings is stored as a scrypt hash and takes precedence over
# this one. Leave both unset only on a machine nobody else can reach: with no
# password, anyone who opens the URL can send messages from your WhatsApp numbers.
# APP_PASSWORD=

# Optional reply tagging. Incoming replies get labelled interested / meeting booked /
# neutral / not interested so the inbox can be triaged, and a label you set by hand is
# never overwritten. One key is enough: choose the provider in Settings, where you can
# also pick the model by name and read or edit the exact prompt used to classify a
# reply. Any text-in, text-out chat model works. Tagging is best-effort, so a bad key
# or a provider outage leaves replies untagged and never interrupts sending.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Only read when the provider is Claude and no model has been chosen in Settings.
# Kept for installs that set it before the model became a setting.
# WA_AI_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# Development only: replaces WhatsApp with a stub, so nothing real is ever sent and no
# number can be put at risk. `npm run test:flow` refuses to run without it.
# WA_DRY_RUN=1
